UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Comparing unit fractions', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part. Compare by thinking about equal-part size.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOrder least to greatest: 1/2, 1/8, 1/3 (same wholes).\n\nFEEDBACK\n1/8; 1/3; 1/2.\n\nWATCH FOR\nComparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCompare twelve pairs of unit fractions with diagrams or number lines. Write <, > or = and a one-line reason naming part size.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part. Compare by thinking about equal-part size.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Strategies","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-PF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Compare twelve pairs of unit fractions with diagrams or number lines. Write <, > or = and a one-line reason naming part size. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for compare; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions.. Correction uses 1/8; 1/3; 1/2.. Why: restores accurate compare.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses compare with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with compare, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for compare, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about compare, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for compare. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","1/8; 1/3; 1/2","Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions"],"question":"Order least to greatest: 1/2, 1/8, 1/3 (same wholes). Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for compare; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part","1/8; 1/3; 1/2","Careful use of unit fraction and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with compare?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named unit fraction and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions","Name unit fraction and explain why it matters only, without linking it to compare","Apply compare to new material and name the unit fraction and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies compare with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on compare?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught compare method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore units and treat 1 and 2, as interchangeable","1/8; 1/3; 1/2","Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions","Read 1 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value"],"question":"Using the method for compare, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a compare problem with numbers such as 1 and 2,, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same compare method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 1 and 2, as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions"],"question":"Solve a fresh compare problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions","For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part","1/8; 1/3; 1/2","Label units when comparing 1 with 2"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for compare?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for compare, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses compare with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for compare; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about compare, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with compare, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions.. Correction uses 1/8; 1/3; 1/2.. Why: restores accurate compare.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w14-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise compare.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use compare with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify unit fraction and explain why it matters.","I can practise same whole independently on a new prompt.","I can notice denominator needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n1/2 > 1/5 on the same whole because halves are larger pieces than fifths. 1/8 < 1/3 for the same reason.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOrder least to greatest: 1/2, 1/8, 1/3 (same wholes).\n\nFEEDBACK\n1/8; 1/3; 1/2.","explain":"For unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part. Compare by thinking about equal-part size.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Unit fraction comparison on the same whole.","caption":"Larger denominator → smaller unit part.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Larger part</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1/2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Smaller part</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1/5</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: 1/2 > 1/5 on the same whole because halves are larger pieces than fifths. 1/8 < 1/3 for the same reason. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: 1/2 > 1/5 on the same whole because halves are larger pieces than fifths. 1/8 < 1/3 for the same reason. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n1/2 > 1/5 on the same whole because halves are larger pieces than fifths. 1/8 < 1/3 for the same reason. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Correct unit order contrasted with digit-size trap.","caption":"Compare part size, not digit size.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Order</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1/8; 1/3; 1/2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Trap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">thinking 1/8 &gt; 1/3</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nComparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions. Here is a more productive model: 1/2 > 1/5 on the same whole because halves are larger pieces than fifths. 1/8 < 1/3 for the same reason.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n1/8; 1/3; 1/2.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Comparing numerators only (thinking 1/8 > 1/3 because 8>3) reverses the truth for unit fractions. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about compare with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFor unit fractions of the same whole, the larger denominator means a smaller part. Compare by thinking about equal-part size. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 2: Comparing unit fractions","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Fractions","week":14,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n1/2 > 1/5 on the same whole because halves are larger pieces than fifths. 1/8 < 1/3 for the same reason.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Unit fraction comparison on the same whole.","caption":"Larger denominator → smaller unit part.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Larger part</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">1/2</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Smaller part</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1/5</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Correct unit order contrasted with digit-size trap.","caption":"Compare part size, not digit size.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\